{"name":"Cacao","hex":"#6b5848","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Cacao","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Malvaceae – synonym of Theobroma."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A barangay of Hilongos, Leyte, Philippines."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Cacao"}],"entries":[{"title":"Theobroma cacao","blocks":[{"text":"Theobroma cacao is a small evergreen tree in the Malvaceae family. Its seeds—cocoa beans when dried and fermented—are used to make chocolate liquor, cocoa powder, cocoa butter and chocolate. Although the tree is native to the tropics of the Americas, the largest producer of cocoa beans in 2022 was Côte d'Ivoire."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Matadecacao.jpg/330px-Matadecacao.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theobroma_cacao"},{"title":"Cocoa bean","blocks":[{"text":"The cocoa bean, also known as cocoa or cacao, is the dried and fully fermented seed of Theobroma cacao, the cacao tree. Due to its high fat content, the bean can be ground into a liquor, from which cocoa butter and cocoa powder are extracted. Cacao trees are native to the Amazon rainforest. They are the basis of chocolate and Mesoamerican foods including tejate, an indigenous Mexican drink."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e0/Cocoa_Pods.JPG/330px-Cocoa_Pods.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cocoa_bean"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Theobroma_cacao2.jpg/500px-Theobroma_cacao2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Theobroma_cacao2.jpg/500px-Theobroma_cacao2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thu